00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.190
as thorough as possible, and
yet, as concise as possible.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.190
Our first question
is about motivation,

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.830
in particular, how to
maintain motivation

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.730
over long periods of time.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.450
This was the question
asked by Martin Zokov.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.120
He wrote, "I alternate
between periods

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.940
of two different states
that vary from a few weeks

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.320
to a couple of months.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.290
I have extremely high
motivation in one state,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.850
where I can do multiple things--

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.000
side projects, making music,
as well as my main things,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.570
or really low-motivational
states, where I can barely

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.270
do anything, and I only look
for short term entertainment."

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.430
I'm guessing, short-term
entertainment comes

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.860
in the form of video
games, social media,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.220
and just doing generally
unproductive things,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.080
as we all do from time to time.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.900
He goes on to write, "What would
be the best set of protocols

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.000
to normalize those
extremes into a more

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.070
stable and consistent state?"

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.540
Well, first off, this is
an excellent question.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:19.170
I say that because it's a
question that I hear a lot,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.220
and I think that many people
are interested in knowing how

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.960
motivated they ought to feel.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.540
And I think a lot of
people also feel a lot less

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.012
motivated than they would like.

00:02:29.012 --> 00:02:30.720
Now, here the question
was, specifically,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.600
about how to not go from
these extremes of days

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.310
or weeks of high
motivation to days

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.990
or weeks of low motivation.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.120
But before we do that, we
need to take a step back

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.260
and acknowledge that, just as
with anxiety, or happiness,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.030
or sadness, we as human beings
don't have an objective window

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.323
into how other people
experience motivation.

00:02:50.323 --> 00:02:51.990
In fact, most of the
time, we don't even

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.312
realize how we
experience motivation.

00:02:54.312 --> 00:02:55.770
We just know whether
or not we feel

00:02:55.770 --> 00:03:00.210
a high barrier or a low
barrier to leaning into work

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.210
and getting things done.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:02.910
In fact, I have
a good friend who

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.130
did many years in the
special operations community,

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.990
and then went on to
the finance community,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.390
and then now works in health
and wellness community.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.360
He has a great mental image
for all of us to adopt.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.690
It's certainly one that
I've adopted, which is--

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.150
for anything in our
life, we can either

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.490
be back on our heels,
flat footed, or forward

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:21.210
center of mass.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.627
Back in our heels, meaning
really struggling; flat footed,

00:03:23.627 --> 00:03:26.170
meaning we're doing OK;
or forward center of mass,

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.580
meaning that we feel as if
we're really tackling things

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:32.100
and that we are in
control of our environment

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.990
or at least to some degree.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.490
So I place that
imagery in your mind

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.050
because I'll return to
it a little bit later

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.250
in the question when
we get into some

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.900
of the underlying
circuitry and tools.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.630
In the meantime, I want
to remind everybody what

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.090
the basis of motivation is.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.150
There are many neurochemicals
and neural circuits involved

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.810
in what we call motivation,
but a central theme

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.180
of the neuroscience
of motivation

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.570
is that the neural modulator
dopamine is involved.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.798
Now, dopamine does other things
besides control motivation.

00:04:00.798 --> 00:04:02.340
In fact, it controls
light adaptation

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.860
in the retina, that is your eye.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.570
It controls a number
of different things

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.520
in terms of movement.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.360
It controls all sorts of things,
but it is strongly related

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.070
to the motivation pathways.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.040
How do we know that?

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.373
Well, there are experiments
on animals and humans, which

00:04:17.373 --> 00:04:20.610
show that even in the
absence of dopamine,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.640
or in the presence of very
low dopamine, I should say,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.910
people and animals can
still experience pleasure.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:30.090
However, when dopamine
levels are too low,

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.450
people's ability
to pursue pleasure,

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.270
or their willingness to pursue
pleasure, in particular,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.750
their willingness to undergo
effort to pursue pleasure

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.900
or any goal of any
kind, not just pleasure,

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.330
any goal of any
kind, is strongly

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.110
regulated by the
levels of dopamine.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.990
So if dopamine
levels are too low,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.240
people simply will
not put in the effort

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.480
to obtain or reach a goal.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.910
If dopamine levels
are adequately high,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.260
they will put in that effort.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.060
And if dopamine
levels go too high,

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.280
you actually see something
that is pathologic,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.750
which is that people consider
every goal a reasonable goal.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.350
This is often seen in the
manic phase of a manic bipolar

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:10.050
person.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.680
So for instance, somebody
with manic bipolar

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.883
who is in a manic
episode, dopamine levels

00:05:14.883 --> 00:05:16.550
are very, very high,
and they will think

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:17.685
every idea is a great idea.

00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:19.560
And they will have tons
of energy to do that,

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.227
so much so so that
they're not sleeping.

00:05:21.227 --> 00:05:22.860
So obviously, that's
not what we want.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:25.730
What we want, and what the
question asker, Martin,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:31.070
is asking about, is how to keep
dopamine levels in a range that

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:35.420
allow us to lean into effort
but that we don't expend

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.160
our ability to stay motivated.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.900
And we can really
trace that back

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.260
to a biochemical/neural
circuit statement, which

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:46.830
is-- we really want to
control our output of dopamine

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.130
and the baseline
levels of dopamine

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.380
from which that output is taken.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.830
In other words, we want to think
about dopamine as a reservoir

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.750
or residing in a reservoir.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.290
That reservoir can be
depleted, so it's exhaustible,

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:04.290
it's depletable, but
it's renewable as well.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.700
And one of the best analogies
that I've ever heard

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.940
was by a previous guest on the
Huberman Lab podcast, Dr. Kyle

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:14.090
Gillett, who's a medical doctor,
obesity specialist, expert

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:14.720
in hormones.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.780
We did an episode on
optimizing hormones

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.280
in males with Dr. Kyle Gillett.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:22.370
You can find that at
hubermanlab.com or anywhere

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:23.720
you can find podcasts.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.170
Dr. Gillett offered an
analogy of the baseline levels

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:30.870
of dopamine as a wave pool,
and I really like this.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.608
So if you think about this pool
full of dopamine-- and here

00:06:33.608 --> 00:06:35.150
we're just talking
about the dopamine

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.067
that resides in the
circuits of the brain that

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:37.880
control motivation.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.370
But that pool of dopamine
you could imagine

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.410
is just sitting there not
doing much of anything

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.407
while you're asleep.

00:06:43.407 --> 00:06:44.990
In fact, while you're
sleeping, you're

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:46.670
replenishing those
dopamine levels.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:48.253
I'll tell you another
tool in a moment

00:06:48.253 --> 00:06:49.770
to replenish those
dopamine levels.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.130
But if you were
to pursue a goal,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.650
really, really go
forward-center of mass

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.620
for many, many hours or many,
many days, in some cases,

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.750
and pursue a goal
or multiple goals

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.300
and you're really driven to do
a ton, what you're effectively

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.060
doing is generating
waves in that wave pool.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:08.150
And if those waves
are too big, well,

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.060
then the waves can't keep
repeating themselves.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.280
So think about the
wave as the motivation

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.680
and the depth of the pool is
the reservoir of dopamine.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.610
And if those waves are too
big, too much excitement, too

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.560
much motivation, too
much center of mass

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.190
for a given period of time,
then the water in this wave pool

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.280
sloshes out of the wave
pool lowering the reservoir.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.840
And then there are
really three ways

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.550
that you can replenish
that reservoir,

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:35.330
and you want to maintain
or replenish that reservoir

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:36.680
if it's been depleted.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:37.740
How do you do that?

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.070
Well, first of
all, quality sleep.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:41.630
So when I say
quality, I mean where

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.340
you're getting enough slow
wave sleep and rapid I

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:43.923
move in sleep.

00:07:43.923 --> 00:07:46.705
So for some people, six hours,
for some people, eight hours.

00:07:46.705 --> 00:07:48.580
Some people might even
need a little bit more

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:49.730
or a little bit less.

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.050
We have episodes-- the
Perfect Your Sleep episode,

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.300
the Master Your Sleep episode.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:54.467
We have a toolkit for sleep.

00:07:54.467 --> 00:07:56.630
All available at zero
cost at hubermanlab.com,

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:57.360
links et cetera.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.360
So check those out for
getting your sleep right.

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.850
But sleep is really
when you replenish

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.510
that reservoir of dopamine.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:05.442
So you cannot ignore sleep.

00:08:05.442 --> 00:08:06.900
I'll come back to
this in a moment.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.350
The second science
supported tool

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.030
that's really been
shown to replenish

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.940
dopamine, in particular,
dopamine within the pathways

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:17.060
that regulate motivation, is
a practice I've talked about

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.250
before on the podcast
called non-sleep deep rest,

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:21.512
sometimes called yoga nidra,
although, yoga nidra is

00:08:21.512 --> 00:08:22.470
a little bit different.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.890
There are two studies
out of Denmark

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.730
that have explored yoga nidra
in the context of dopamine.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.770
The first one simply involved
having people do a yoga nidra

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:32.270
practice.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:33.978
Again, this doesn't
involve any movement,

00:08:33.978 --> 00:08:36.440
but it involves people,
potentially you,

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:40.010
doing anywhere from 30 to 60
minutes, although there are now

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:41.809
data showing that as
short as 10 minutes

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:45.680
of a non-sleep deep rest,
a.k.a. yoga nidra protocol,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.040
leads to dramatic,
really dramatic increases

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:50.780
in striatal dopamine reserves.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:52.760
So it essentially
is replenishing

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.470
the dopamine reserve pool.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:57.530
This is why I'm such a
fan of using NSDR, a.k.a.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:00.680
yoga nidra, at least once a
day and especially under times

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.970
when you're engaging in
a lot of high output.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.145
And when I say,
especially at times

00:09:05.145 --> 00:09:07.020
when you're engaging in
a lot of high output,

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.770
this is a mistake
many people make.

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:11.502
They push, push,
push, push, push.

00:09:11.502 --> 00:09:12.710
They're in pursuit of a goal.

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:14.960
Then they hit that point
where they're exhausted.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.900
Then they start doing
all the dopamine reserve

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:22.280
pool replenishing tools
such as yoga nidra or NSDR.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.790
The real key is
to always tap off

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:29.630
that or refill that
reservoir once a day

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.760
before it's completely depleted.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.170
Now, this gets into some of
the biochemistry of dopamine

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:37.880
and the relevant circuits,
but it takes a lot longer

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.540
to restore the
dopamine reservoir--

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.100
think of it still
as a wave pool,

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:46.250
but that reservoir from a
place of complete depletion

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:47.990
then it does of
partial depletion.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:50.000
So there's an asymmetry
in the way this is done.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.265
So it's not as if you
drink a glass of water.

00:09:52.265 --> 00:09:54.140
You fill the glass of
water at a certain rate

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:55.730
and it fills up to
a certain level,

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:56.970
and the rate is constant.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:01.280
Think about it as once the level
of dopamine in your reserve

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:03.390
is depleted past
a certain point,

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:08.110
it takes a lot more effort,
much more sleep, much more NSDR,

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.870
things of that sort to
replenish that reservoir.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.700
Now, oftentimes
what people will do

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.710
when they start
feeling less motivation

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.830
is they will start relying
on things like Adderall,

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:21.550
Ritalin, some cases
illegal substances that

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:22.740
can increase dopamine.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:23.740
You know what those are.

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:25.780
Please don't ever
lean in to those.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.420
They are extremely dangerous.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:30.340
They really are because
of their ability

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.080
to potently release dopamine.

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.422
And guess what, deplete
that reservoir even further.

00:10:35.422 --> 00:10:36.880
We've talked about
some supplements

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.010
on the podcast that
can replenish dopamine,

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.140
L-tyrosine in particular.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.780
Mucuna pruriens is
actually 99% l-DOPA,

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:45.460
the precursor to dopamine.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.580
I don't necessarily
recommend Mucuna pruriens.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.980
It tends to make people
very dopaminergic--

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.230
drive, drive, drive, drive,
motivated, and then crash.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.090
Again, depleting that pool.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:56.590
L-tyrosine is a
little bit milder.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.260
But I really encourage
people to lean first

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.510
on the behavioral
tools such as an NSDR.

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:05.080
And by the way, there's a NSDR
script, totally zero cost,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.770
that you can find by putting
my name and NSDR into YouTube.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:12.640
That one works quite well if you
are looking for a short NSDR.

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:14.110
There are some other NSDRs.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.450
You can simply look on
the internet or YouTube

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.730
and just put NSDR
and you'll find NSDR.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:21.270
Or if you prefer to do
the more classic yoga

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.770
nidra type approach, there are
a lot of different yoga nidra

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.930
options to choose
from on YouTube.

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:28.780
Many people think NSDR
or yoga nidra are simply

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:31.270
meditation with a body scan.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:32.830
That's not true.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:36.010
Meditation is a focus exercise.

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:37.840
Most meditations
are focus exercise.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.730
NSDR restores energy
through the dopamine system,

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.620
and newer data are
starting to show

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.815
that it can actually
recover lost sleep,

00:11:46.815 --> 00:11:48.190
so if you're not
sleeping enough.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:52.480
But to return to NSDR, a.k.a.
yoga nidra, as a practice,

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.420
yes, it's been shown in
laboratory studies, in humans,

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.740
by the way, to restore
dopamine levels.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.740
There's another
study, lesser known,

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:03.040
from that same group that
was published in 2011,

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:06.940
which is entitled Dopaminergic
stimulation enhances confidence

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.650
and accuracy in seeing
rapidly presented words.

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.290
This was a cognitive task.

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:14.620
They explored yoga nedra, a.k.a.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:18.400
NSDR, in the context of
increasing striatal dopamine.

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:20.590
They already knew that it
did that, so that's great.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:23.170
They confirmed that result.
But what they also found

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:28.270
is that doing NSDR could restore
confidence in cognitive ability

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:30.620
and performance in
these cognitive tasks.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:32.530
So this is a really
powerful, zero cost

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.380
tool for re-upping
or replenishing

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:36.760
that dopamine reserve.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:40.690
So this is something to do every
day, especially when you're not

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:41.750
feeling depleted.

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.695
So the question,
again, was about how

00:12:43.695 --> 00:12:45.820
to make sure that you don't
go through these cycles

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.220
of extreme motivation and
then lesser motivation.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.450
Well, get your sleep right.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:51.460
I always say, 80% or more
of the nights of your life,

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:53.950
hopefully the nights that it's
not good, are for good reasons

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.200
that you're enjoying yourself.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.000
But hey, life happens,
so 100% of the time

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.160
it's just not reasonable
to expect of yourself.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:04.360
Do NSDR once a day
for either 10 minutes.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.670
If you have the time to do
20-30 minutes or an hour,

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:09.288
you will see even
more positive effects.

00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:11.080
It has been shown in
these research studies

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:12.913
to replenish dopamine,
levels of confidence,

00:13:12.913 --> 00:13:15.933
cognitive ability, et cetera,
and sense of motivation.

00:13:15.933 --> 00:13:17.350
And I said there
were three tools,

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:19.630
and the third tool
that really can

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:23.920
allow you to keep the
dopamine, a.k.a. motivation

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:27.460
circuitry, tuned up
properly is to really start

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:30.220
paying attention to
peaks in dopamine

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:33.400
and be very careful
about layering

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:36.820
in too many things that can
stimulate the dopamine system.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:39.070
I talked about this quite
a bit in the episode

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:44.560
that we did on ADHD and
building and maintaining focus.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.600
There are many things
out there nowadays

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.890
that will deplete
the dopamine system.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.940
For instance-- and by the way,
none of what I'm about to list

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:53.030
is necessarily bad.

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:55.120
I actually used some
of these things.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:58.395
For instance, caffeine will
increase dopamine receptors

00:13:58.395 --> 00:13:59.770
that will allow
whatever dopamine

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:01.720
is available to be more potent.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.573
OK, so caffeine is
great for some people,

00:14:03.573 --> 00:14:04.990
less good for
people with anxiety.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:05.920
Don't drink it too
late in the day

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:07.670
because it will interfere
with your sleep,

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:09.140
and so on and so forth.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:13.963
But many people will
combine caffeine with music

00:14:13.963 --> 00:14:15.130
that they particularly like.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:15.910
Music's great.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:18.070
Music can stimulate dopamine
release, we know this.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:19.570
It can enhance
motivation, especially

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:20.860
if is the kind of music
that really puts you

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.630
in the groove for the
particular type of work

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:23.422
you're going to do.

00:14:23.422 --> 00:14:26.710
For me, I like to listen to
either loud fast music or Glenn

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:28.990
Gould classical piano,
so one or the other.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:31.040
I know what's right for
me for a given time.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:32.320
You'll know what's right
for you for a given

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:33.520
time and your preferences.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.070
But what will happen is people
will start consuming caffeine

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:37.727
at higher and higher levels.

00:14:37.727 --> 00:14:39.310
Again, caffeine isn't
necessarily bad,

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:40.518
but they'll start doing that.

00:14:40.518 --> 00:14:42.980
And they'll start layering
it in, or stacking,

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.440
very potent music,
potent for them,

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:47.540
plus things like L-tyrosine.

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:50.018
Again, none of these things
are terrible on their own.

00:14:50.018 --> 00:14:51.560
In fact, they can
be very beneficial.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:53.540
Sometimes they'll start
taking Mucuna pruriens.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:54.950
Sometimes they'll
start relying on things

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:56.000
like Adderall, Ritalin.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.502
And pretty soon
what's happening is

00:14:57.502 --> 00:14:59.960
they're getting these big waves
in that dopamine wave pool,

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:00.980
big peaks.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:03.800
And within a few days or
maybe even within a few hours,

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:05.750
they're depleted and
they're at that low.

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:07.400
And then, as Dr.
Anna Lembke, who

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:09.943
is a guest on the podcast,
talked about in terms

00:15:09.943 --> 00:15:12.110
of addiction but also in
her wonderful book Dopamine

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:17.330
Nation, what happens is after
those big peaks in dopamine,

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:19.610
the reservoir, the
baseline in dopamine,

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:22.260
drops below its initial level.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.650
So it's as if the reservoir
got deeper, and it's emptier,

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:27.470
and it takes much,
much longer to fill.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:30.620
So to be quite specific,
what I'm recommending

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:32.750
is get your sleep right.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:34.790
Ideally, every night of
your life, but for as

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:36.620
many nights of your
life as possible.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:38.750
That's clearly replenishing
dopamine and sense

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:39.680
of motivation.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:42.380
Do all the things associated
with that-- morning, sunlight,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:44.260
lack of artificial
light at certain hours

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:45.260
of the night, et cetera.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:47.635
All of that's in the Toolkit
for Sleep and other episodes

00:15:47.635 --> 00:15:48.650
I mentioned before.

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:52.370
Have a practice that
is research supported

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.500
to replenish dopamine,
and incorporate

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:55.940
that practice any time of day.

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:59.168
Again, NSDR can be done
morning, afternoon, or evening,

00:15:59.168 --> 00:16:00.710
or middle of the
night if you wake up

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:01.790
and you need to
get back to sleep,

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:03.320
it can be very
beneficial for that.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.840
But do it as a
consistent practice

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:09.890
so that dopamine reservoir
remains tapped off.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:15.680
And as a third point, please be
wary of, or at least aware of,

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.570
these peaks in
dopamine and the fact

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:20.270
that layering in a lot of things
that stimulate dopamine, well,

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:22.950
that can be wonderful for your
wedding, birth of a new child,

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.950
going to a sports event
with a bunch of friends,

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.210
celebrating a big anniversary.

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:29.240
Yes, please do
celebrate and enjoy

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.180
the wonderful events of life,
but please also understand

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.840
and expect there will be a
lull, a sort of postpartum low,

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.520
maybe not full blown
depression, that

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:40.010
follows that unless
you incorporate

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:42.560
some tools and practices
to replenish that dopamine.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:45.200
Does that mean you should never
combine caffeine, L-tyrosine,

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:47.270
music, and a workout,
and time with friends?

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:49.010
No, absolutely not.

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.290
But don't expect to
do that, and then

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:53.210
go do an intense bout
of work, and then get up

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:55.670
the next morning and do
it all over again for more

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:58.618
than a few days before you
find yourself pretty depleted.

00:16:58.618 --> 00:17:00.410
So rather than give
you a specific schedule

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.810
of do seven days of this
and four days of this, what

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:06.047
I encourage you to do is, for at
least five days a week-- maybe

00:17:06.047 --> 00:17:08.339
give yourself some time off
on the weekends, maybe not.

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:10.400
But for at least
five days a week,

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.740
get into a consistent
routine that

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:15.980
is, I should say,
neurobiologically consistent as

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.650
well with how the
dopamine, a.k.a.

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:20.450
circuits that control
motivation, work.

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:22.910
And I assure you that
you will find yourself

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:25.790
in a more regular groove
of focus, and attention,

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:27.829
and alertness, and
motivation when you need to.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:30.230
And provided you're
doing all the things

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:32.330
I described, and
hopefully paying attention

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.790
to other things like nutrition
and social connection too,

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:39.200
of course, you'll find a much
more even pattern of motivation

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.190
over time.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:43.580
One last thing before I conclude
the answer to this question.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:47.540
When I was in graduate school,
I got some wonderful advice

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.427
from an excellent neurologist.

00:17:49.427 --> 00:17:50.510
His name is Robert Knight.

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:52.677
He used to be at University
of California, Berkeley.

00:17:52.677 --> 00:17:54.560
I think he's retired
now but is still

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:56.100
active in the
scientific community.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.560
And I asked him what he
was doing that weekend.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:17:59.810
I don't know why this came up.

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:01.268
And he said, oh,
I'm going fishing.

00:18:01.268 --> 00:18:02.505
I like mindless recreation.

00:18:02.505 --> 00:18:03.380
I said, that's great.

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:04.422
You know, fishing is fun.

00:18:04.422 --> 00:18:06.737
I'm not particularly
into fishing myself,

00:18:06.737 --> 00:18:08.570
but I've done it a few
times and I enjoy it.

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:12.050
And he said, the most important
thing for a science or medicine

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.150
career or any demanding career?

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:14.780
I said, what?

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:18.110
I was all ears, super
hungry to get in the mix

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:19.730
and do research
and publish papers.

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:22.820
And he said, figure out
how many hours a day you

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:26.280
can do real work consistently.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.790
That means five days a week,
for some people six or seven,

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:31.980
but five days a week I
think for most people

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.010
is going to be a bit healthier
overall for your social life

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:36.030
and family, et cetera.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.670
And he said, figure
that out, and know

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:42.240
that that number is what you
should apply over, and over,

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:46.440
and over again, but update that
number about every four or five

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:47.060
years.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:49.560
And I said, OK, so does that
mean that over time I'm working

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:50.852
more and more or less and less?

00:18:50.852 --> 00:18:53.610
And he said, ah, here's the
deal-- as you get better

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.740
at your profession,
you will find

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:59.610
that you can do more potent
work, more directed work,

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:02.130
in a shorter amount
of time, but that

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:03.870
does not mean that
you can continue

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:06.730
to expand the amount of time
that you're doing focused work.

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:07.870
In fact, the opposite.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:09.810
So this follows a sort
of general principle

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:12.480
that's also present in
resistance training,

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:14.280
weightlifting, right?

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:16.890
The analogy there is
that people always

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:19.560
imagine that as you get better
and better at resistance

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.180
training that you should
do more and more volume,

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:22.170
just keep adding volume.

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:23.880
And there's some
evidence to support that.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.280
More volume for hypertrophy
as opposed to less, et cetera.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:27.447
We've done episodes on this.

00:19:27.447 --> 00:19:29.460
However, there's a
different school of thought

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.050
that works exceedingly
well, and it

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.620
runs in the exact
opposite direction,

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:35.580
which is as you get better
at controlling muscular

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:36.720
contractions--

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:38.670
or let's say for in
an endurance sport,

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:42.480
as you get better at regulating
your stride, and breathing,

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:43.950
and all those
things, you actually

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:49.230
can do more "adaptation"
stimulating damage

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:50.880
during a given training session.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.520
So you want to train
less not more over time

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.710
because beginners don't
actually have the ability

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:59.010
to get much done in a lot of
time or a short period of time,

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.260
whereas, experts can come
in there and really nail it.

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:05.280
So I think that advice that
Robert Knight was really key,

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.060
and it's something that I've
followed throughout my career.

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:09.180
So at one period of my life,
I won't mention the hours

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.230
that I worked in
graduate school,

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.000
they were pretty
insane to be honest.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.540
I had family members
get a little concerned.

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:14.957
I actually lived
in the laboratory

00:20:14.957 --> 00:20:16.500
even as a junior professor.

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:18.540
I don't suggest people
do that by the way,

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:20.250
but I enjoyed it at the time.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:23.250
And the key thing is
that you figure out

00:20:23.250 --> 00:20:26.347
what you can do consistently
and still maintain mental health

00:20:26.347 --> 00:20:27.180
and physical health.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:28.350
That's key as well.

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.260
And do that, and then,
every couple of years or so,

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:33.810
update that,
typically, by reducing

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.310
the total amount
of time that you're

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:36.960
doing that high-potency work.

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:39.030
I think that, combined
with the other tools

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:40.830
that I described
before for generating

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:44.460
ongoing dopaminergic circuits,
keeping that reservoir full,

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.290
ought to give you
consistent motivation.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:49.057
Again, it's an art, and a
practice, and a science,

00:20:49.057 --> 00:20:51.390
so don't expect to get it
perfect the first time around.

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:54.562
But I wish you all luck, and I'm
certain that these tools work.

00:20:54.562 --> 00:20:56.520
Thank you for joining
for the beginning of this

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:57.960
ask me anything episode.

